# BELOW THIS LINE IS GENERATED, and the ordering is the point: CODEOWNERS is
# last-match-wins, so an organization's line has to come after the `*` rule
# above or the `*` rule overrides it and the organization owns nothing.
#
# `backend/app/core/codeowners.py` writes it from the roster: one line per
# claimed organization, naming its registry directory and the GitHub accounts
# its approved codeowners have linked. An organization whose codeowners have
# linked no account is LEFT OUT rather than given an empty line - a pattern
# with no owners after it overrides the `*` rule and requests nobody, which is
# worse than being covered by the maintainer.
#
# ONE THING THIS CANNOT DO. GitHub reads CODEOWNERS from a pull request's BASE
# branch, so a pull request that adds its own ownership line is not gated by
# it. An organization's first registry pull request therefore cannot also be
# the one granting it ownership: one pull request adds the line and the empty
# directory, and every registry change after that is gated by the owners it
# established. Doing both at once would leave the first import - the change
# that decides what an organization's trails are - as the one nobody at that
# organization has to approve.
#
# WHAT THESE LINES DO TODAY: REQUEST, NOT REQUIRE. "Require review from Code
# Owners" is OFF, decided 2026-09-21, so an organization's approval is asked
# for and does not block the merge - the maintainer still merges by hand,
# having seen it. The lines are still doing real work: last-match-wins means
# a pull request touching an organization's registry requests review from
# THAT organization rather than only from the maintainer, which is how its
# codeowners find the pull request at all.
#
# WHY THE GATE IS OFF, AND WHAT TURNING IT ON WOULD COST. Code-owner review
# is its own requirement, independent of the approval count - so
# `required_approving_review_count: 0` is not what stands in the way. The
# `*` rule above is. It matches every file, so with the requirement on,
# every pull request would need the maintainer's approval, and GitHub has no
# toggle that lets an author approve their own (see
# .github/expected-protections.yml, which records that platform rule and
# that it applies to agent-authored pull requests too, the token being the
# maintainer's). Turning the gate on therefore means deleting or narrowing
# the `*` line first, and merging that to main BEFORE the setting is
# flipped - the other order locks every open pull request at once.

